---

<ProjectLayout
	projectName="CauSyncVLA"
	navItems={[
		{ label: "Abstract", href: "#abstract-heading" },
		{ label: "In motion", href: "#video-heading" },
		{ label: "Method", href: "#method-heading" },
		{ label: "Evaluation", href: "#evaluation-heading" },
		{ label: "Results", href: "#results-heading" },
		{ label: "Citation", href: "#citation-heading" },
		{ label: "References", href: "#references-heading" },
	]}
	meta={{
		title: "CauSyncVLA",
		ogImage: "/social/projects/causyncvla.png",
		description:
			"Causal latent world modeling and predictive flow warm-starting for asynchronous robot control.",
	}}
>
	<article class="project-page">
		<ProjectHero
			year="2026"
			title="CauSyncVLA: Causal Latent World Modeling for Asynchronous Robot Control"
			{authors}
			{affiliations}
			notes={[{ mark: "*", text: "Corresponding author" }]}
			venue={{ label: "Under review" }}
			{links}
		/>

		<section class="project-section" aria-labelledby="abstract-heading">
			<div class="section-label"><span>01</span><h2 id="abstract-heading">Abstract</h2></div>
			<p class="project-abstract">
				Vision–language–action policies must react while visual inference is still running.
				Overlapping inference and execution reduces waiting, but leaves perceptual memory stale and
				successive action chunks disconnected. CauSyncVLA couples a causal latent world model, which
				updates delayed perceptual memory from executed state–action history, with predictive flow
				warm-starting, which refines the unexecuted plan in one step. On LIBERO-10, it reaches 93.8%
				success with 5.04× the model replanning capacity of a ten-step synchronous baseline on a
				shared GPU.
			</p>
			<figure class="wide-figure">
				<div class="figure-media">
					{
						corners.map((corner) => (
							<span class={`figure-cross figure-cross--${corner}`} aria-hidden="true">
								+
							</span>
						))
					}
					<Image
						src={teaserImage}
						alt="Synchronous and naive asynchronous control compared with CauSyncVLA's coupled perceptual-memory prediction and action correction"
						loading="eager"
					/>
				</div>
				<figcaption>
					<strong>Fig. 1.</strong> CauSyncVLA aligns fast feedback control with slower visual updates,
					using execution history to predict perceptual memory and an unexecuted plan to warm-start trajectory
					correction.
				</figcaption>
			</figure>
		</section>

		<section class="project-section" aria-labelledby="video-heading">
			<div class="section-label"><span>02</span><h2 id="video-heading">In motion</h2></div>
			<figure class="video-figure">
				<video
					controls
					playsinline
					preload="metadata"
					aria-label="Real-robot demonstrations comparing pi 0.5 and CauSyncVLA"
				>
					<source src="/projects/causyncvla/real-robot.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
					Your browser does not support embedded video.
				</video>
				<figcaption>
					Real-robot rollouts compare synchronous {"$\\pi_{0.5}$"} and CauSyncVLA on three-cup stacking,
					fruit placement, and vegetable sorting. The trials have independently reset scenes.
				</figcaption>
			</figure>
		</section>

		<section class="project-section" aria-labelledby="method-heading">
			<div class="section-label"><span>03</span><h2 id="method-heading">Method</h2></div>
			<p class="project-abstract">
				A slow visual stream publishes key–value (KV) memory anchors, while a fast control stream
				propagates those anchors to the current execution time and refines the action horizon. The
				backbone and action expert remain frozen; only the lightweight latent predictor is trained.
			</p>
			<figure class="wide-figure">
				<div class="figure-media">
					{
						corners.map((corner) => (
							<span class={`figure-cross figure-cross--${corner}`} aria-hidden="true">
								+
							</span>
						))
					}
					<Image
						src={methodImage}
						alt="CauSyncVLA architecture showing the causal latent world model, action-sensitive distillation, and predictive flow warm-starting"
						loading="lazy"
					/>
				</div>
				<figcaption>
					<strong>Fig. 2.</strong> Architecture and training: executed state–action history conditions
					KV-memory prediction, and a shifted, rebased action tail initializes single-step flow refinement.
				</figcaption>
			</figure>
			<div class="method-grid">
				<article>
					<span>01</span><h3>Causal latent world model</h3><p>
						A predictor updates delayed KV anchors using the state transitions and commands actually
						executed since the anchor was captured.
					</p>
				</article>
				<article>
					<span>02</span><h3>Action-sensitive distillation</h3><p>
						In addition to KV reconstruction, the frozen expert's response supervises the predictor
						to preserve memory features that matter for control.
					</p>
				</article>
				<article>
					<span>03</span><h3>Predictive flow warm-starting</h3><p>
						The unexecuted action tail is aligned to the current state and refined in one flow step,
						without training the action expert again.
					</p>
				</article>
			</div>
			<div class="method-explanation">
				<h3>Execution-grounded control</h3>
				<p>
					At a replanning boundary, the latest visual anchor {"$z_a$"} was captured at time {"$a$"},
					while the robot is now at {"$t$"}. The history {"$\\mathcal H_{a:t}$"} contains realized state
					transitions and executed commands; {"$\\mathcal Q_t$"} is the unexecuted tail of the preceding
					plan, shifted past consumed commands and rebased to the current state. Both records therefore
					refer to actual execution.
				</p>
				<div class="equation" tabindex="0">{equations.control}</div>
				<p>
					The latent model predicts a residual to the delayed KV anchor; the age gate leaves a
					freshly captured anchor unchanged. Causality here means using information available by the
					decision time. It does not imply prediction of future pixels, or identification of
					external physical dynamics.
				</p>
				<h3>Predictive flow warm-starting</h3>
				<p>
					Instead of drawing a new trajectory from pure noise, PFWS mixes the shifted prior with
					Gaussian noise at flow time {"$\\rho$"}, then applies one Euler step of the frozen action
					expert:
				</p>
				<div class="equation" tabindex="0">{equations.warm}</div>
				<p>
					Smaller {"$\\rho$"} retains more of the prior trajectory, while {"$\\rho=1$"} recovers cold-start
					sampling. The experiments use {"$\\rho=0.5$"}; no additional action-expert training is
					required.
				</p>
				<h3>Action-sensitive distillation</h3>
				<p>
					During training, the fresh anchor {"$z_t$"} is a teacher for the prediction {
						"$\\hat z_t$"
					}. Both query the same frozen expert with identical noise, flow time and state. KV
					reconstruction preserves the representation, and the action term prioritizes errors that
					change the expert's output:
				</p>
				<div class="equation" tabindex="0">{equations.loss}</div>
				<p>
					Here {"$\\hat A$"} and {"$A^\\star$"} are the student and detached teacher outputs; {
						"$m$"
					} masks valid KV entries and {"$w$"} weights action coordinates. Only the latent predictor is
					updated.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="project-section" aria-labelledby="evaluation-heading">
			<div class="section-label"><span>04</span><h2 id="evaluation-heading">Evaluation</h2></div>
			<p class="project-abstract">
				The controller builds on a frozen {"$\\pi_{0.5}$"}<sup class="citation-mark"
					><a href="#ref-1">[1]</a></sup
				> backbone. We evaluate 500 official starts on LIBERO-10<sup class="citation-mark"
					><a href="#ref-2">[2]</a></sup
				>, 2,519 paired perturbation variants on LIBERO-Plus<sup class="citation-mark"
					><a href="#ref-3">[3]</a></sup
				>, and 100 shared starts under a 20 Hz, 26-second closed-loop budget. Physical evaluation
				uses a Franka FR3 arm with two cameras on cup stacking, held-out fruit placement, and
				vegetable sorting. Model-only throughput and timed closed-loop response measure different
				aspects of performance. The 24.27M-parameter predictor is trained for 12,000 updates;
				simulation uses horizon 10 and stride 5, while the FR3 experiments use horizon 10 and stride
				2 with live 30 Hz perception. Model capacity excludes simulator and robot execution.
			</p>
			<div class="evaluation-grid">
				<div><strong>93.8%</strong><span>LIBERO-10 success across 500 starts</span></div><div>
					<strong>5.04×</strong><span>shared-GPU model capacity vs. synchronous baseline</span>
				</div><div>
					<strong>86.96%</strong><span>pooled real-robot success across 69 trials</span>
				</div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="project-section" aria-labelledby="results-heading">
			<div class="section-label"><span>05</span><h2 id="results-heading">Results</h2></div>
			<p class="project-abstract">
				CauSyncVLA reaches 93.8% native success while reducing mean batch-one model latency from
				60.01 ms to 11.91 ms against the ten-step synchronous baseline. Under the separate timed
				closed-loop protocol, mean per-episode median observation-to-first-action response drops
				from 176.73 ms to 83.04 ms. PFWS improves continuity, but its benefits under perturbation
				depend on the type of shift: large viewpoint or layout changes can favor cold starts.
			</p>
			<figure class="paper-table-figure" data-sync-table-caption>
				<div
					class="table-scroll"
					tabindex="0"
					role="region"
					aria-label="LIBERO-10 task competence and model inference capacity"
				>
					<table class="paper-table">
						<thead
							><tr
								><th scope="col">Controller</th><th scope="col">Decoder</th><th scope="col">H/C</th
								><th scope="col">Success (%) ↑</th><th scope="col">Mean (ms) ↓</th><th scope="col"
									>P95 (ms) ↓</th
								><th scope="col">Plans/s ↑</th><th scope="col">Actions/s ↑</th></tr
							></thead
						>
						<tbody
							>{
								rows.map((row) => (
									<tr class={row.ours ? "paper-table__ours" : undefined}>
										<>
											<th scope="row">
												{row.name}
												{row.ref && (
													<sup class="citation-mark">
														<a href={`#ref-${row.ref}`}>[{row.ref}]</a>
													</sup>
												)}
											</th>
											<td>{row.decoder}</td>
											<td>{row.horizon}</td>
											<td>
												{row.ours ? (
													<strong>{row.success}</strong>
												) : row.name === "VLA-Rolling" ? (
													<u>{row.success}</u>
												) : (
													row.success
												)}
											</td>
											<td>
												{row.ours ? (
													<strong>{row.mean}</strong>
												) : row.name.includes("+ PFWS") ? (
													<u>{row.mean}</u>
												) : (
													row.mean
												)}
											</td>
											<td>
												{row.ours ? (
													<strong>{row.p95}</strong>
												) : row.name.includes("+ PFWS") ? (
													<u>{row.p95}</u>
												) : (
													row.p95
												)}
											</td>
											<td>
												{row.ours ? (
													<strong>{row.plans}</strong>
												) : row.name.includes("+ PFWS") ? (
													<u>{row.plans}</u>
												) : (
													row.plans
												)}
											</td>
											<td>
												{row.ours ? (
													<strong>{row.actions}</strong>
												) : row.name.includes("+ PFWS") ? (
													<u>{row.actions}</u>
												) : (
													row.actions
												)}
											</td>
										</>
									</tr>
								))
							}</tbody
						>
					</table>
				</div>
				<figcaption class="table-caption">
					<strong>Table 1.</strong> LIBERO-10 task competence and batch-one model inference capacity.
					Success uses 500 starts and a 520-action budget; timing measures model inference rather than
					timed closed-loop execution. Bold and underline mark the best and second-best values; H is the
					action horizon and C is the replanning stride.
				</figcaption>
			</figure>
			<h3 class="result-heading">Model capacity and closed-loop response</h3>
			<p class="project-abstract">
				Reducing the synchronous expert from ten flow steps to one lowers mean latency from 60.01 ms
				to 36.00 ms. Moving visual encoding off the fast path reduces the shared-GPU measurement
				further to 11.91 ms. The resulting 5.04× is model replanning capacity, not a measured
				fivefold increase in robot dispatch. Under the separate 20 Hz timed protocol, measured
				replans rise from 2.57 to 3.28 per second and completion rises from 86% to 91% across 100
				shared starts.
			</p>
			<figure class="result-figure">
				<div class="figure-media">
					{
						corners.map((corner) => (
							<span class={`figure-cross figure-cross--${corner}`} aria-hidden="true">
								+
							</span>
						))
					}<CapacityChart />
				</div>
				<figcaption>
					<strong>Fig. 3.</strong> Batch-one model replanning capacity normalized to ten-step synchronous
					{"$\\pi_{0.5}$"}, at stride 5. Shared GPU runs visual encoding concurrently; isolated
					System 1 removes this contention.
				</figcaption>
			</figure>
			<figure class="paper-table-figure" data-sync-table-caption>
				<div
					class="table-scroll"
					tabindex="0"
					role="region"
					aria-label="Single GPU timed closed-loop execution"
				>
					<table class="paper-table">
						<thead
							><tr
								><th scope="col">Controller</th><th scope="col">Success (%) ↑</th><th scope="col"
									>Response P50 / P95 (ms) ↓</th
								><th scope="col">Replans/s ↑</th><th scope="col">Actions/s ↑</th></tr
							></thead
						>
						<tbody>
							<tr
								><th scope="row">{"$\\pi_{0.5}$"}</th><td>86.00</td><td><u>176.73</u> / 189.97</td
								><td><u>2.57</u></td><td>13.02</td></tr
							>
							<tr
								><th scope="row">Latent Bridge R0</th><td>82.00</td><td>183.88 / <u>189.79</u></td
								><td>2.48</td><td>12.58</td></tr
							>
							<tr
								><th scope="row">VLA-Rolling</th><td><u>87.00</u></td><td>295.21 / 345.94</td><td
									>1.28</td
								><td><u>13.20</u></td></tr
							>
							<tr class="paper-table__ours"
								><th scope="row">CauSyncVLA</th><td><strong>91.00</strong></td><td
									><strong>83.04 / 90.53</strong></td
								><td><strong>3.28</strong></td><td><strong>16.60</strong></td></tr
							>
						</tbody>
					</table>
				</div>
				<figcaption class="table-caption">
					<strong>Table 2.</strong> Timed execution over 100 shared starts at 20 Hz with a 26-second budget.
					Policy inference and rendering share one RTX 5090; response values are means of per-episode
					P50 and P95.
				</figcaption>
			</figure>
			<h3 class="result-heading">Does execution history preserve useful memory?</h3>
			<p class="project-abstract">
				On 320 held-out windows, stale anchors yield action MSE 0.10355 against fresh-anchor expert
				outputs. Conditioning on ordered execution history reduces it to 0.00983. Age and endpoints
				help, but lose intermediate motion; shuffling state–action pairs retains much of the
				geometric signal while degrading the ordered model. These are prediction errors within the
				frozen expert, not a direct test of physical dynamics.
			</p>
